# Key server test: a key server that lies (an old head, an invented head, "no head"). The lies are made by changing the key server's record file for one user while the key server is stopped; its code is unchanged.
# Date 2026-11-06 (06:37 to 06:41; the SDK warning lines carry the times). uid cecil14. Pasted terminal transcript of both terminals, terminal 2 (the manager) first, terminal 1 (the key server) after it.
# Jar: the build of 26 Oct 2026, whose source is identical to tag thesis-final; honest client edu.sydney.e2se4j.pm.PasswordManager (head on); key server on the laptop; real S3; no adversary program (the storage is honest throughout).
# Names: PM is the manager's command line of Appendix A.3; REC is the key server's record file for this user, kstate/<SHA-256 of the user id>.properties. The passphrase came from the environment variable E2SE_PP, so no prompt appears.
# Order of every change to the record: the key server was stopped (^C in terminal 1), the file was changed in terminal 2, the key server was started again (it reads the record files only when it starts).
# Edits to the paste: the test passphrase is replaced by [test passphrase] and the key server's port number by [port]. Nothing else was changed or removed.
# One command was typed before the key server had been started again, the first retrieve after the fingerprint was replaced: "could not unlock", exit 1, no file. It was repeated once the key server was up.
# Outcome: (1) an old genuine head put back (version 1 while storage holds version 2): retrieve refused, "storage has version 2, key server says 1", exit 4, no file. (2) under that old head a store from a copy at version 1 was accepted, the key server moved the head 1 -> 2 a second time, and the earlier version 2 was replaced: a lost update, exit 0, and the next retrieve returned the latest write, not an old vault. (3) the fingerprint in the head replaced by an invented one: retrieve refused, "same version number 2, different bytes", exit 4, no file. (4) the head removed: retrieve and list stopped with "no vault stored yet", exit 1; a store from a copy with a receipt was refused as a conflict, exit 3; a first add stored a new vault of one entry as version 1 over the stored one, exit 0: the second route of the lost update. In no case was an old vault shown as current.
